# The one file this repository takes credentials and machine-specific settings from.
#
#   cp .env.example .env      # then fill in what you need; .env is gitignored and never committed
#
# Who reads it, and how:
#
#   apps/api   `./gradlew bootRun` and `./gradlew test` — build.gradle.kts loads this file into both.
#              **Your shell wins**: a variable already exported is used as exported, so a one-off
#              `MPRELIST_STRIPE_SECRET_KEY= ./gradlew bootRun` still overrides what is written here.
#   infra/     `docker compose --env-file .env -f infra/docker-compose.yml up -d --wait`, from the
#              repository root. The flag is not optional — without it compose looks for a `.env` beside
#              the compose file, finds none, and starts with every variable below unset.
#
# Nothing in the npm workspaces reads it yet. The day one does, point Vite's `envDir` at this file's
# directory rather than starting a second .env beside it.
#
# One `NAME=value` per line. Quotes are stripped and protect a `#` inside them; in an unquoted value ` #`
# starts a comment. Compose and the Gradle loader read this file the same way, which is asserted by matching
# their output rather than assumed — the divergence would otherwise be a webhook secret that verifies nothing.
#
# **A variable you have no value for is commented out, never written empty.** Compose passes an empty
# assignment to Keycloak as a variable that is *defined and empty*, which is not the same as absent: the realm
# file's own `${MPRELIST_SMTP_HOST:sandbox.smtp.mailtrap.io}` default applies only to the absent case, so
# `MPRELIST_SMTP_HOST=` would import a realm with no mail host at all. The two Stripe secrets below are the
# exception, along with the client-config document, because empty is what `application.yml` defaults them to
# anyway — and for that document empty is not a gap to fill but the ordinary state of a healthy deployment.
#
# `EnvironmentFileTest` holds this file to what actually reads the environment: a setting that arrives in
# `application.yml` or in the compose file and is not named here fails the build, so does a variable named
# here that nothing reads, so does an empty assignment for anything the realm substitutes, and so does a
# real-looking secret pasted into this example.


# --- The extension id, which the realm is built around ------------------------------------------------

# T44 pinned the extension's id with a `key` in apps/extension/public/manifest.template.json, and this is
# what that key derives to — recompute it with `npm run -s extension-id -w @mprelist/extension`, which is
# where the value below came from. A test derives it from that same key, so this line cannot go stale
# quietly.
#
# **It has to be right before the stack's first `up`.** Keycloak imports the realm on first start only, and
# the realm registers `https://<this id>.chromiumapp.org/` as the extension's redirect URI; getting it wrong
# is a `docker compose ... down -v` and a fresh import, not a restart. The API also reads it, as the browser
# origin it answers, where being wrong costs only a restart.
#
# At publish the Web Store assigns a different id (#87) and this is one of the two places it changes.
MPRELIST_EXTENSION_ID=dolloiambeojifmnofjchofbnepodiof

# Where `apps/web` is served from, which the API answers as a browser origin — the admin corner (T54, #85) is
# the first thing on that site that calls the API at all, and a browser refuses its calls before this
# application sees them if the origin is not allowed.
#
# The Vite dev server's address locally, and the same value `mprelist.web.base-url` is built from, since one
# is the origin of the other; a test holds the two together. At Go-Live it becomes the deployed origin (T55,
# #86) and the realm has to register it too (T56, #87). Being wrong costs a restart, not a `down -v`.
MPRELIST_WEB_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173

# And where a browser is *sent* — the same site, spelled as a URL rather than as an origin, because that is
# what a Stripe Checkout session's `success_url` takes and what the Customer Portal returns a browser to.
#
# Two variables rather than one because they are two different things and `CrossOriginTest` holds them
# together, so neither can move alone: a deployment that changed this and not the origin above would have a
# working payment flow and an admin corner no browser will call, which is a pair of symptoms nobody connects.
MPRELIST_WEB_BASE_URL=http://localhost:5173


# --- Stripe (test mode) -------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The test-mode secret key from the Stripe dashboard, which `GET /api/plans` reads amounts with and
# `POST /api/checkout` opens sessions with. Unset, both answer as though Stripe were unreachable — the plan
# cards say "unavailable, retry" rather than inventing an amount — and the API says so once at startup.
#
# Never a live key here. Nothing in this repository has been run against one, and the click-through is
# written for test mode throughout (docs/click-through.md, slice C).
MPRELIST_STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=

# The signing secret of the webhook endpoint, and the whole of the authentication on the one route no bearer
# token reaches. `stripe listen --forward-to localhost:8080/api/stripe/webhook` prints one per session, so
# this changes every time you start it; a deployed endpoint has its own, in the dashboard.
#
# Unset, the API starts, says so at WARN, and refuses every event — which is the only safe reading of "we
# cannot tell whether this is Stripe", and is why a developer working on something else needs no Stripe
# account at all.
MPRELIST_STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=


# --- The extension's own configuration, for the day Marktplaats moves something ---------------------------

# What the extension should do differently from what it was built to do (ADR-0013, #36): Marktplaats's
# internal paths, how the CSRF token is found, and the gap the client leaves between two relists. Served by
# `GET /api/client-config` and layered over the defaults built into every release.
#
# **Empty is the normal state**, and means "the defaults are correct" — which is what a healthy deployment
# serves, and why this is safe empty like the two Stripe values above. It earns its keep on the day every
# installed extension breaks at once because a path moved: the fix is this variable and a restart, instead of
# a Web Store review that takes days.
#
# The whole document on one line, as JSON. Only the fields being overridden, and never `schemaVersion` —
# that one names the shape the API serves and is stamped by it:
#
#   MPRELIST_CLIENT_CONFIG={"endpoints":{"deleteAd":"/seller/api/delete-ad-v2"}}
#
# A document the API cannot read stops it from starting, deliberately: the moment to find out is the deploy,
# not the next relist. See `mprelist.client-config` in apps/api/src/main/resources/application.yml.
MPRELIST_CLIENT_CONFIG=


# --- Mail, for the one thing in the realm that sends any ------------------------------------------------

# Keycloak sends a password-reset mail for a self-registered user and nothing else. Unset, it accepts the
# reset and fails to send, which is a line in the container log rather than anything a user sees. A Mailtrap
# sandbox inbox is what infra/README.md describes; the host, port and from-address have defaults in the realm
# file and the two credentials have none worth having.
#
# Read at the stack's first `up`, like everything else the realm imports. Commented out rather than left
# empty, for the reason at the top of this file: an empty assignment reaches Keycloak as a defined-and-empty
# variable, and the realm's defaults — the Mailtrap sandbox host and port — apply only where it is absent.
# Uncomment the pair you need; the sandbox inbox gives you both.
# MPRELIST_SMTP_HOST=sandbox.smtp.mailtrap.io
# MPRELIST_SMTP_PORT=2525
# MPRELIST_SMTP_FROM=no-reply@mprelist.local
# MPRELIST_SMTP_USER=
# MPRELIST_SMTP_PASSWORD=


# --- Your machine, not this project ---------------------------------------------------------------------

# Docker Desktop for Linux only. Testcontainers starts Ryuk first, which mounts the Docker socket into
# itself, and Desktop's socket lives on a path its VM will not share — every container then fails with
# `mounts denied: the path /socket_mnt/...`. This is where that same socket is inside the VM.
#
# Commented out because it is a property of your daemon: set on a plain Docker Engine it points at the right
# place anyway, but it is nobody else's default. This file is per-machine and gitignored, which is what makes
# it the right home for it — see apps/api/README.md.
# TESTCONTAINERS_DOCKER_SOCKET_OVERRIDE=/var/run/docker.sock
